From f035fcd0e98b36d923957a9c17e35e5c00c36912 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Cipoletti <adam@amcreativecoach.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 22:48:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Engine: the style invalidator popped a stack past its bottom

Invalidator::invalidateStyleForTree walks a subtree keeping a stack of
ancestors, and when it moves sideways or upwards it pops that stack until
the top is the current element's parent:

    while (parentStack.last() != parent) {
        parentStack.removeLast();

The outer test three lines above guards last() with isEmpty(). This one
does not. If the parent is not on the stack at all, the loop empties it
and then asks an empty Vector for its last element, which computes an
index of zero minus one.

GCC's -fisolate-erroneous-paths, which -O2 turns on, sees that the index
can underflow and replaces the path with a trap, so the process dies
cleanly at a known address rather than reading whatever lies before the
buffer. That is how it was found: a PowerBook G4 took SIGTRAP inside this
function with a signed-in Gmail message on screen, and the disassembly
around the faulting address computes size - 1 and size - 2 and branches
to the trap when the size is zero.

Both engine builds contain that trap, so this is not something an
optimisation introduced. WebKit 2.52 still writes the loop the same way,
so it is not an upstream fix that was missed either: the invariant has
simply held everywhere anyone ran it.

Stopping at the bottom of the stack costs nothing when the invariant
holds, and when it does not it leaves the selector filter's stack in step
with the traversal's, since both now stop together. A stale filter for
the remainder of a subtree is a wrong style at worst; the alternative is
an out-of-bounds read.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Source/WebCore/style/StyleInvalidator.cpp | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Source/WebCore/style/StyleInvalidator.cpp b/Source/WebCore/style/StyleInvalidator.cpp
index 31432ba8..71d80041 100644
--- a/Source/WebCore/style/StyleInvalidator.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/style/StyleInvalidator.cpp
@@ -156,7 +156,25 @@ void Invalidator::invalidateStyleForTree(Element& root, SelectorFilter* filter)
                 if (filter)
                     filter->pushParent(parent);
             } else {
-                while (parentStack.last() != parent) {
+                // The stack is popped until it reaches this element's parent,
+                // on the understanding that the parent is always somewhere on
+                // it. When that does not hold the loop empties the stack and
+                // then asks an empty Vector for its last element, which is an
+                // index of zero minus one.
+                //
+                // GCC sees that and turns the path into a trap, so instead of
+                // reading whatever lies before the buffer it crashes cleanly
+                // in Invalidator::invalidateStyleForTree - which is what a
+                // PowerBook G4 did while a signed-in Gmail message was on
+                // screen. WebKit 2.52 still has the loop written this way, so
+                // this is not a fix that arrived upstream and was missed; the
+                // invariant simply held everywhere it was tried.
+                //
+                // Stopping at the bottom of the stack keeps the traversal's
+                // own bookkeeping consistent with the selector filter's,
+                // because both stop together, and turns a crash into at worst
+                // a stale filter for the rest of this subtree.
+                while (!parentStack.isEmpty() && parentStack.last() != parent) {
                     parentStack.removeLast();
                     if (filter)
                         filter->popParent();
